# scripts/research/nonmutable/nixos.capabilities — R2 PROTOTYPE, not a fleet declaration
# (NON-MUTABLE-HOST-PROPOSAL.md §5 R2, #1004). What a dotfiles-NixOS would declare — a
# repo that does not exist yet; the new-os-repo.sh starter ran clean on a NixOS 25.05
# guest (research-nonmutable-vm.yml, runs 34819015395 and 34820719480). Written for a
# CHANNEL-managed box; a flake-managed one swaps two verbs (noted inline).
#
# The R2 test, against the schema rather than a sibling (there is none):
#   PKG_INSTALL / PKG_REMOVE
#                 a NixOS box DOES have an imperative install: `nix-env -i` into the
#                 user's profile, which persists across `nixos-rebuild switch` (the user
#                 profile is not the system configuration). It is the documented
#                 anti-pattern — the declarative way is `environment.systemPackages` —
#                 but it is a TRUE verb, and the newer `nix profile install nixpkgs#…`
#                 cannot take the schema's space-separated argument. Declared, with the
#                 caveat; core-doctor's hint would say "or declare it in configuration.nix".
#   PKG_UPGRADE   `nixos-rebuild switch --upgrade` refreshes root's channel, rebuilds and
#                 ACTIVATES — no PKG_APPLY; the one family where the mutable assumption
#                 "returns with the box updated" holds. (Flakes: `nix flake update` in
#                 the config directory, then `nixos-rebuild switch --flake .`.)
#   PKG_COUNT_PENDING
#                 THE ONE REQUIRED KEY WITH NO TRUTHFUL VALUE. "Packages pending" is not a
#                 thing a declarative host knows; the nearest question — what would a
#                 rebuild change? — is `nixos-rebuild dry-build --upgrade`, which needs
#                 root's channel (sudo) and lists derivations, not packages. So the key is
#                 ABSENT, which the prototype validator allows under
#                 PROVISIONER=declarative, and `up` already reads an absent count verb as
#                 the -1 sentinel that keeps the nudge silent. That relaxation is the
#                 schema's one concession to this family.
#   PKG_OWNS      `nix-locate` answers "which package provides this path" but needs the
#                 nix-index database; declared with that caveat — the same shape as
#                 `dnf provides` needing dnf's metadata.
# Verdict for this family: additive, with one required key made conditional — a validator
# rule, not a re-author for the nine.

PROVISIONER=declarative

# ── package-manager verbs (required) ──────────────────────────────────────────
PKG_REFRESH=sudo nix-channel --update
# Rebuilds AND activates: no reboot, no PKG_APPLY. The kernel is the exception (a new
# kernel activates at the next boot), which `nixos-rebuild` itself says.
PKG_UPGRADE=sudo nixos-rebuild switch --upgrade
# Imperative, per-user, persists across rebuilds. See the header for why not
# `nix profile install`.
PKG_INSTALL=nix-env -i
PKG_REMOVE=nix-env -e
# `nix search` needs the nix-command feature on a box that has not enabled it.
PKG_SEARCH=nix --extra-experimental-features nix-command search nixpkgs
# Needs nix-index (`nix-index` once, then nix-locate reads its database).
PKG_OWNS=nix-locate --top-level
# PKG_COUNT_PENDING — deliberately absent; see the header.

# ── scheduler (required) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Measured: systemd user units work on the guest; the declarative alternative is
# `systemd.user.services` in the configuration, which a repo could ship instead.
SCHEDULER=systemd
SCHEDULER_UNIT_DIR=~/.config/systemd/user

# ── how `up` applies (optional) ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# No PKG_ASSUME_YES (nixos-rebuild does not prompt). No PKG_UPGRADE_PARTIAL (a
# configuration switches as a whole). No MAINT_UNATTENDED_UPGRADE: NixOS has
# `system.autoUpgrade`, declared in the configuration, and Core's runner must not run a
# second one beside it.

# ── the login shell on this host (measured, for the driver's arm) ─────────────
# `chsh -s zsh` TOOK on the guest (users.mutableUsers defaults to true), and the next
# activation REVERTS it — measured, run 35133704599, across a real second generation.
# The nuance is what makes the rule worth stating: `mutableUsers` leaves UNDECLARED users
# alone (an imperative `useradd` account kept its chsh'd shell) while rewriting the shell of
# every DECLARED user back to the declared value on each activation. So the hand edit
# survives exactly where nobody needs it to, and is reverted on the operator's own account.
# `/etc/shells` is not regenerated to include the new shell either, and `chsh` warns
# "invalid shell" for a path outside it while still returning 0 — a warning, not a refusal.
# The declaration the driver should print instead of running chsh:
#   users.users.<name>.shell = pkgs.zsh;  programs.zsh.enable = true;
